WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.720
Film Courage: Pam, when you were taking The
Adderall Diaries as the memoir and then adapting

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:11.570
it into a screenplay, how much did you embellish
the drug use and sex scenes or did you try

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:12.570
to tone them down a bit?

00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.780
Pamela Romanowsky: The drug use and the sex
scenes are actually toned down quite a bit.

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.020
I think that this was something that Stephen
(Elliott-The writer of the memoir) was very

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:28.240
interested in for the book and I was less
interested in for the film because for me

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:34.820
this film was about the relationships between
these characters and how consequences in those

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:41.829
relationships led to a realization in the
main character, so his unusual sex life and

00:00:41.829 --> 00:00:46.610
his pretty moderate drug use are both parts
of that and part of who he is.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:51.500
But to me, those are sort of just elements
of a character.

00:00:51.500 --> 00:01:00.059
And I think that S&amp;M is all over the media,
all over cinema right now and so to me it

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:05.290
wasn’t as shocking as it was in maybe 2008
when he was writing the book (and in 2009

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:07.729
when the book came out).

00:01:07.729 --> 00:01:11.530
So it seemed pretty easy to me to accept that
it’s a part of this guy’s life and the

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:15.130
Adderall use is a part of his life and we
didn’t really need to talk about it that

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.109
much, it’s just part of who he is.

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:22.250
Film Courage: James, I think you’ve said
previously that Stephen writing the book was

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:25.319
not just a memoir, he was trying to create
a piece of art?

00:01:25.319 --> 00:01:27.899
James Franco: (Recalling) O.k.

00:01:27.899 --> 00:01:30.719
I think I know what you’re getting at.

00:01:30.719 --> 00:01:34.539
Film Courage: How much do you feel about this
in your own work?

00:01:34.539 --> 00:01:37.020
Because there are a lot of similarities between
the two of you.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.789
You’re both very passionate, you go against
the grain, you’re both writers.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:48.419
James: I think I was trying to define what
the book was more, meaning it’s not just

00:01:48.419 --> 00:01:49.950
a straight memoir.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:59.319
This book is not just “Then I did this….then
I did this…then I did this…”

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:07.100
There’s a more complicated kind of structure
or approach here where the way that he’s

00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:18.860
remembering things, it is focused on and he
starts to question or highlight that process

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:25.590
in his self and extend it to all of us in
that we edit and tell our stories in a particular

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.480
way, both to ourselves and to others.

00:02:31.480 --> 00:02:35.680
For me, the books that I’ve written are
categorized as fiction.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:45.990
I certainly use some of my own experiences
in those books, but they are very much fiction,

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.129
so I think I’m doing something a little
different.

00:02:48.129 --> 00:02:49.129
Film Courage: Right.

00:02:49.129 --> 00:02:53.349
One of my favorite quotes is actually that,
to step out of our own story and then once

00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:56.569
we do we see the world differently and the
people around us differently.

00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:01.569
Did the two of you experience this in your
own lives, having watched Stephen and his

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:05.239
process and whether his story was totally
true or not, did you have any of your own

00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:07.050
revelations about your lives?

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:14.030
Pam: Part of the appeal for me in telling
this story was the opportunity to look at

00:03:14.030 --> 00:03:19.109
places in my own life where my narrative doesn’t
match someone else’s.

00:03:19.109 --> 00:03:24.849
I think everybody tells the story of a break-up
differently or a relationship with a parent

00:03:24.849 --> 00:03:25.939
and that’s fascinating.

00:03:25.939 --> 00:03:29.579
It’s not malicious, it’s not flawed, it’s
part of who we are.

00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:33.909
We’re narrative machines and we need to
make sense of who we are and we need to make

00:03:33.909 --> 00:03:37.069
sense of what’s happened to us.

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:40.349
Film Courage: James?

00:03:40.349 --> 00:03:52.790
James: My story about myself goes up in conflict
with sort of a public persona almost all the

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:53.840
time.

00:03:53.840 --> 00:04:03.029
I am a public personality and often times
I don’t think that what is projected out

00:04:03.029 --> 00:04:04.959
there is me.

00:04:04.959 --> 00:04:12.849
But I’ve come to accept that as somebody
who sort of lives half of his life in public.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:19.250
I know that I can’t control that version
of myself, that story that is being told by

00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:23.650
a big collective or through a lot of different
outlets.

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:27.400
It certainly contrasts with who I think I
am.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:33.990
But I also have kind of reconciled myself
with it, that it’s just part of my life.

